      "writtenOn": "2018-12-11T22:29:52Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "From what I understand: your load_fifo function loops through samples it got from the IMU side fifo, calls this push_fifo_data function for each.\n\nThe issue is that you directly use last_interrupt_timestamp, since last_interrupt_timestamp might change at any point before you\u0027re finished (unless it\u0027s impossible for the IMU to raise another irq before this all samples are done).\n\nAt some point before you allow the IMU to re raise the IRQ again, you have to save last_interrupt_timestamp, and use it for all samples that you read in one batch.\n\nIt\u0027s ok if you skip an irq timestamp. As long as if you have a group of samples with repeat timestamps, the timestamp has to match the first sample in that group.\n\nHere\u0027s a good example:\n(interrupt, record t0)\nfirst call of load_fifo yields: [(s0, t0), (s1, t0), (s2, t0)] (interrupt, record t3)\nsecond call of load_fifo yields: [(s3, t3), (s4, t3), (s5, t3)] (interrupt, record t5)\n\nWhat the kernel sees:\n[(s0, t0), (s1, t0), (s2, t0)]\n[(s3, t3), (s4, t3), (s5, t3)]\n\nNote how (t0, s0) and (s3, t3) correspond.\n\nWhat your code will produce:\n(interrupt, record t0)\nfirst call of load_fifo yields: [(s0, t0), (s1, t0), (interrupt, record t3) (s2, t3)]\nsecond call of load_fifo yields: [(s3, t3), (s4, t3), (s5, t3)] \n\nWhat the kernel sees:\n[(s0, t0), (s1, t0)]\n[(s2, t3), (s3, t3), (s4, t3), (s5, t3)]\n\nThe kernel will now spread the samples wrong, will make s0, s1 more spread out, s2-s5 more dense.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 134,
        "startChar": 7,
        "endLine": 134,
        "endChar": 31
      },
